    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Cloud storage makes data available through a remotely managed service, with object storage providing a documented example in Iran. Understanding objects, access permissions, versioning, and delivery integration helps distinguish storage features from evidence of recoverability or service performance.",
    "tags": ["Cloud storage", "Object storage", "Iran", "Data management", "Backup"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T19:14:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T19:14:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Cloud Storage in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore object storage, permissions, versioning, and documented Iranian storage offerings, with clear limits on performance claims."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "storage-model",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From files to objects",
        "content": "Object storage is a useful starting point for understanding cloud storage. Amazon's documentation describes an object as a file together with metadata describing it, held inside a container called a bucket. A key identifies an object within that bucket. This terminology explains a widely encountered storage model; Amazon's documentation is used here for technical context, not as evidence that its services are available to customers in Iran. [s1]\n\nThe distinction matters when reading Iranian product descriptions. ArvanCloud's object-storage page presents storage for website content, images, service media, logs, and backup files. It describes access through the web or an API, meaning an application programming interface through which software can manage data. These examples show how storage can serve an application directly rather than functioning only as a personal folder for uploaded documents. They remain statements about the provider's offering. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "versions-and-permissions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Keeping data and controlling access",
        "content": "Versioning retains different versions of an object instead of treating every replacement as the only surviving copy. Amazon documents this as a way to restore objects accidentally overwritten or deleted. Its documentation also separates storing an object from granting access to it: permissions determine who may use the stored resource. These are distinct questions, and understanding both is essential to interpreting a storage service. [s1]\n\nArvanCloud advertises versioning, lifecycle rules for removing objects, and client-side or server-side encryption. Its page also describes storing data in other data centers and integrating buckets with content delivery settings. These features concern different parts of data management: retaining earlier states, controlling retention, protecting stored information, and making content accessible. The inspected material does not demonstrate how these features performed in a recovery exercise or establish that every advertised option was available under every plan. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "storage-boundaries",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the storage promise does not establish",
        "content": "A storage feature list is not sufficient evidence of an organization's ability to recover its information. NIST's public-cloud guidance identifies security and privacy challenges when data and services move outside an organization's own environment. Applied to storage, that framing makes the boundary between the customer and provider a central part of the subject: the location of the service, access arrangements, and documented responsibilities all require examination. This is an editorial application of the guidance, not a legal conclusion about Iran. [s3]\n\nFor this page, the most defensible conclusion is limited but useful. Public documentation demonstrates that an Iranian provider markets programmable object storage with data-management and delivery features. It does not establish national storage capacity, independently measured durability, or a complete history of service availability. A fuller account needs dated documentation and operational evidence, particularly before presenting advertised resilience as an observed outcome. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
