    "description": "A content delivery network distributes web content through servers that can reuse cached responses closer to users. Iranian providers market CDN services alongside security and traffic-management features, but product claims alone do not establish nationwide coverage or measured improvements.",
    "tags": ["CDN", "Iran", "Web performance", "HTTP caching", "Content delivery"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T19:14:42Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T19:14:42Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "CDN Services in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "How content delivery networks work, what Iranian providers advertise, and why caching rules and measured performance matter."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "delivery-overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Bringing content closer to readers",
        "content": "A content delivery network, usually shortened to CDN, uses geographically distributed servers to deliver web content. Cloudflare's technical explanation describes caching content near end users and distinguishes the CDN from the web host that supplies the original content. Images, stylesheets, scripts, and other website assets can be delivered through this intermediate network. A CDN therefore adds a delivery layer to a website rather than automatically replacing its underlying application or hosting service. [s1]\n\nThe original source of content is called the origin. An edge server is part of the delivery network that handles requests nearer to users. Reusing an eligible cached response can reduce repeated trips to the origin, but the benefit depends on whether the requested content can actually be reused. This distinction connects the physical distribution of servers with the rules governing the responses they deliver. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "caching-rules",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Caching is a controlled process",
        "content": "The Internet Engineering Task Force's HTTP caching standard distinguishes shared caches, which can serve multiple users, from private caches dedicated to one user. Reuse depends on conditions including the request, relevant headers, freshness, and validation. A response that is fresh can sometimes be reused without checking the origin again; another response may require validation first. [s2]\n\nThese distinctions matter for websites that mix public material with personalized pages. The standard defines directives governing storage and reuse, including restrictions associated with private responses and sensitive information. A CDN's ability to cache content is consequently not a reason to treat every response alike. Correct behavior depends on the response rules and the cache configuration, not simply on enabling a delivery service. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iranian-offerings",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What Iranian product pages document",
        "content": "ArvanCloud's Persian CDN page advertises content caching, file and image optimization, domain-name management, traffic reporting, and security controls. These controls include rate limiting, which restricts request volume, and a web application firewall, or WAF, intended to identify and block unwanted web requests. The page establishes the advertised range of functions; it does not independently demonstrate protection against every attack or a particular improvement in loading speed. [s3]\n\nParsPack also markets a CDN with caching, compression, domain-name management, firewall rules, and reporting. Its page contains differing statements about the geographic extent of its network, making a precise coverage count unsuitable for repetition without clarification. More broadly, the presence of similar feature names across providers does not establish equivalent configuration options or outcomes. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "delivery-flow",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "A simplified cached-content request",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Reader requests content\"] --> B[\"CDN evaluates request and cached response\"]\n  B -->|\"Reuse is permitted\"| C[\"Return cached response\"]\n  B -->|\"Origin contact is required\"| D[\"Contact origin server\"]\n  D --> E[\"Apply response and caching rules\"]\n  E --> F[\"Return response to reader\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows describe request processing, not ownership or legal authority. The simplified flow omits errors and detailed validation exchanges; a cached response is reused only when applicable rules permit it.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpreting-performance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading performance claims",
        "content": "For readers comparing Iranian services, the practical question is how a delivery network performs for the intended audience and content. The inspected product pages are evidence of marketed capabilities, while the technical sources explain why caching can help. Neither combination supplies an independent comparison across Iranian networks, locations, or applications. This page therefore avoids provider rankings and treats coverage and speed claims as matters requiring separate measurement. [s1][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
